hi everyone i'm jakobo simone's friend  the guy who made the previous videos i'm  behind the editing and scripting of some  of them for example the one about  colombia the idea behind this project is  to alternate our voices so in the future  you will often hear my broken english  but let's start our story begins on  april 17 1895. before this date taiwan  had never been under total domination of  a single power for the sake of briefness  we start off with the end of the 19th  century with the treaty of shimonoseki  in 1895 at the end of the first  sino-japanese war formosa as taiwan was  called and the pangu islands were given  inconcession to japan japan was very  interested in this islands as he wanted  to affirm its position as a colonizing  country so in reaction to what many saw  as a betrayal by china some nobles loyal  to the qing dynasty set up the so-called  republic of taiwan to resist without  success to the new rulers this episode  will provide one of the arguments used  by the current independentists against  the prospect of a new unification with  mainland china however with the advent  of the land of the rising sun any  connection with china was interrupted to  make room for the new demands and  political objectives of the emperor the  new occupants presented themselves as  the benefactors who would raise the  cultural level of a taiwan that  according to them was an urgent need of  reform in a few decades japanese rule  was instrumental in making taiwan a  modern colony in every aspect and on  this today most taiwanese agree japanese  imperialism is no longer seen as a dark  parenthesis to be forgotten but a  fundamental stage in the country's  progress which gave it a separate status  from both the expanding japanese empire  and mainland china in fact  infrastructures were improved public  hospitals were built the first paved  roads bridges big power plants and even  a railway line it was an all-round  modernization which also interested the  rural economy with the birth of the  first chemical industries to refine  sugarcane into sweetener and facilitate  the export on the japanese market after  the second world war the defeat of japan  meant that taiwan as a result of the  cairo declaration of november 1943  returned under chinese sovereignty and  precisely under the control of the  republic of china which was none other  than the kuomintang nationalist party  led by chang jeshi who was appointed as  trustee of the island on behalf of the  allied powers the nationalists  immediately sought tight control over  the economic and political life of the  island due to fear of japanese influence  of the pressures of reconstruction and  of the civil war on the mainland that  opposed them to the communists led by  mao zedong the central government relied  on a provincial administration composed  almost exclusively of mainland personnel  while the taiwanese tried to keep their  autonomy with the self-government a sort  of compromise between formal  independence and complete submission  from late 1945 to mid 1946 the  nationalists increased the tensions  between the provincial administration  and the local population to many china  appeared chaotic and backward as a  potential drain on the island's  resources and the threat to its  stability positioning itself in the eyes  of residents as a new but less competent  colonial ruler reintegration to mainland  china meant recolonization something the  taiwanese wanted to avoid at all costs  in early 1947 tensions between state and  society exploded in what became known as  the february 28 incident triggered by  the escape of a cigarette smuggler that  caused police officers who were in not  pursuit to fire shots to break through  the crowd protests in the following days  would allow the taiwanese to quickly  rest control of the island from a nil  prepared the provincial administration  but not for long because after just one  week a military contingent sent from the  mainland crashed all opposition and  massacred thousands of inhabitants from  that moment began the long period of the  so-called white terror which lasted 38  years during which the kmt tried to  suppress even in blood any form of  political descent arriving to imprison  and execute thousands of taiwanese in  december 1949 at the end of the chinese  civil war what was left of the defeated  nationalist party retreated to taiwan  thanks to us intervention that prevented  communists from winning the race  everything seemed to be turning to the  worst but the government was able to  promote a development that today is  known as the taiwanese miracle between  1960 and 1970 thanks to measures such as  rent reduction expansion of education  land reform the introduction of a stock  market and banks that encouraged foreign  investments in taiwanese companies the  value of imports and exports increased  dramatically and the average annual  growth in gross national product  registered the 9.7 percent with a per  capita gdp growth rate of 6.6  unlike the dark period of the 1950s when  income distribution in taiwan was much  like that of mexico or brazil  characterized by a wide gap between the  poorest and richest citizens by 1970  taiwan's income distribution was among  the most equitable in the world better  even than that of the united states it's  not surprising that foreign countries  found in taiwan a valuable resource a  skilled well-educated and highly  motivated workforce who saw in these  measures a unique opportunity to give a  better life to their line of succession  with this in mind the authoritarian  regime had little to fear that at some  point the residents would revolt and on  the off chance that this would happen  law enforcement would promptly be used  to suppress any descent this situation  of unconscious submission let's call it  that would only taper off from the 1980s  onwards when the middle class which had  grown thanks to economic development  began to exert more and more pressure on  the state for a democratic reform in  fact the big problem was that only a  minority of the population participated  in political life precisely 15  corresponding to the military and  civilian elite who came from mainland  china together with chang and  established themselves at the top of all  aspects of society again confining the  locals to a secondary status if we step  back a few years we realized that  stability had begun to falter in 1971  when the republic of china lost its seat  at the united nations to the people's  republic of china and so most states  recognized the latter as the sole  legitimate representative of all of  china and this making a big leap into  the present day brings us to the heated  dispute between taiwan and mainland  china the fundamental question here is  whether taiwan the pangu shinmen and  matsu islands should remain part of the  territory of the autonomous republic of  china or be unified with the territories  now ruled by the people's republic of  china it all depends on the answer to  the following question is it legitimate  to recognize the republic of china as a  sovereign state you need to know that  taiwan is strongly penalized on an  international level we are dealing with  a de facto state that is not recognized  as independent neither by the prc nor by  the other permanent members of the u.n  security council and that on paper  maintains diplomatic relations with the  only 14 states that recognize it beijing  for its part considers it a chinese  province out of its control the result  of the dynamics of the cold war when the  united states prevented mao from  annihilating the nationalists a fact  that allowed them to migrate to the  island and keep the republic of china  alive in order to stabilize relations  between the two sides of the strait  without renouncing the reunification of  the chinese territory beijing has  imposed adherence to the principle of  the existence of a single china the  so-called one-china policy in practice  any state wishing to establish or  maintain relations with the prc must  recognize the china with beijing as the  only existing china and therefore deny  sovereignty to taiwan and not have  official diplomatic relations with it  adhering to this request the vast  majority of countries including the  european union member states maintain  very close but unofficial relations with  taiwan and therefore in many cases  cannot offer taiwanese institutions or  businessmen the same conditions normally  applied to others on the opposite front  taipei authorities claim instead to  represent a fully sovereign government  mind you however that since 1949 when  the constitution of the republic of  china came into force formosa adhered to  the one china principle claiming  sovereignty over the territory now  governed by the people's republic of  china along with outer mongolia but in  1991 after a series of revisions the  kumitanga reduced its claims recognizing  that its powers were limited only to the  areas under its control a further  approachment with the political  trajectory of the prc was then the  adherence to the so-called 1992  consensus whereby beijing and taipei  agreed that there was only one china but  agreed to have a different  interpretation of what said china was  whether the prc or the republic of china  more recently the fear that taiwan could  fall into beijing's orbit has triggered  the mobilization of taiwan's civil  society starting with the more than 3  million young citizens between the ages  of 15 and 25. sea experiences such as  the sunflower movement which in march  2014 marched over 500 000 people leading  to the occupation of parliament and  briefly the seat of government this is  the result of widespread discontent  arising from some important phenomena  such as social inequities rising  unemployment difficulty in finding a  suitable job after studies and  especially the ecological degradation  that plagues several areas of the island  regarding this last aspect of crucial  importance for environmentalists where  the terrible earthquake of 2011 in japan  and the subsequent tsunami that caused  the accident at the fukushima nuclear  plant events to which taiwanese public  opinion and media have given great  prominence given that the island of  formosa is also located in a highly  seismic zone and has at least four  nuclear plants three of which are  located in the municipal area of the  capital tebay in any case disrupting the  status quo in terms of foreign policy  has been the rise of the democratic  progressive party led since 2016 by the  first female president cyinguan her  approach and willingness to reshape the  1992 consensus were seen by beijing as  favorable to a process of independence  and generated an immediate reaction as  in 2017 the southern chinese border  hosted at least 1  500 missiles aimed at the island however  taiwan has those who protect it with the  signing of the taiwan relation act in  1979 in fact washington committed to  defend the sovereignty of the island  providing constant military supplies  that would act as a deterrent for a  possible clash this is because the us  has always considered formosa a  fundamental strategic junction in the  south china sea but how is it possible  that the united states has official  relations with both beijing and taipei  didn't we say that one excludes the  other well yes in theory but in practice  this is possible thanks to a series of  interpretative devices of the agreement  with which the americans have  established diplomatic relations with  the prc back in 1979. in fact the prc  continues to believe that by adhering to  that document the americans have also  implicitly accepted the prc as china  alone but washington doesn't think so  since it has never expressly followed  this interpretation however the us have  managed to not disappoint neither  mainland china nor taiwan on the one  hand they have never expressly supported  taiwanese independence aspirations  meeting the favor of beijing and on the  other hand they have continued to pursue  their policy of deterrence maintaining  close cooperation with taipei the  prospect of a conflict in the strait is  not ruled out although not very  convenient for both actors given the  huge volume of trade between the  taiwanese industries in chinese  territory but if this were to happen the  fate of taiwan would be quite  predictable assuming there is no  interference from outside let's put it  in this way in 2017 chinese military  spending was about 145 billion dollars  and active troops amounted to more than  800 thousands compared to only 130 000  taiwanese not to mention that taiwan's  air force doesn't seem to be able to  compete with china's new chengdu j-20  stealth fighters the navy on the other  hand is the real flagship of the  taiwanese army but it has constant  difficulty in modernizing its  technologies for certain sectors due to  the blockades that beijing imposes on  military companies in 2021 for the  chinese communist party centenary  beijing has again put pressure on taipei  with at least 600 air raids of which 150  in the first week of october alone  seeing that the invasion is imminent is  too difficult to predict according to  the words of the taiwanese defense  minister beijing would have the  capabilities to annex the island by 2025  but not to undermine the sector of  essential goods called semiconductors of  which taiwan is one of the largest  producers for such reason china may  think twice before excluding the  diplomatic route and we are done today i  hope i gave you a clear idea of taiwan's  recent history that's what mattered  we'll definitely be hearing about taiwan  again and again in the coming months see  you in the next video bye

